#> must: # command:  sed  -f
#> not: ERROR parsing
#> source: /^$/d;G
# 2. Double-space a file that already has blank lines. Pement: sed '/^$/d;G'
# Two commands separated by ';'. /^$/ is an address: a pattern that matches
# empty lines, and d deletes them (and starts the next line at once, so G is
# never reached for them). Every remaining line then gets exactly one blank
# line after it, instead of the two that plain G would leave after a blank.
# Try: remove the /^$/d and count the blank lines after Alaska.
/^$/d;G

#DATA
Alabama
Alaska

Iowa
Kansas
Montana
